-- D189/D191/D193: the FIRST source program that mentions the ν half of the
-- language. Before this branch none could: the type grammar had `Mu` and no
-- `Nu`, so no annotation could name a final coalgebra, so no `ana` could be
-- written — and an unreachable feature cannot fail a test, which is exactly
-- how a vacuous `obs-correct-Out` and a FALSE `obs-correct-Ana` survived.
--
-- What this exercises, end to end:
--   * `Nu ( K Int )` through the verified parser (`pa-nu`, D191)
--   * `ana` through `checkAna` (D192), with the functor read off the
--     annotation
--   * `Surface.ana` → `elaborate` → `curry (Ana wfF … ∘ snd)`
--   * `ir-to-trace'`'s `Ana` clause (D189): the ten-instruction two-cell
--     suspension build plus the coalgebra as a named block
--   * assembly and linking of that block
--
-- What it does NOT exercise: FORCING. `Out` has no surface syntax yet (D194
-- is parked one lemma short), so the ν is built and discarded. The companion
-- `nu-ana-force.once` is the real guard, and it waits on D194.
--
-- Expected: exit code 42.

import I.Linux.Syscalls as S

-- The coalgebra of a degenerate functor: `⟦ K Int ⟧T Int = Int`, so a
-- coalgebra `Int → ⟦ K Int ⟧T Int` is just `Int → Int`, and `id` is one.
-- The ν it unfolds to has a single layer and no recursive positions — the
-- `Mu (K Int)` test's mirror.
mkNu : Int -> Nu (K Int)
mkNu = ana id

-- The ν must be REACHED from `main`, or the def is elided and this tests only
-- the frontend: with `mkNu` unused the emitted `.text` is byte-identical to a
-- program without it. Consuming it through `terminal` is the most that can be
-- done until `Out` exists — it forces the suspension to be BUILT (the ten
-- instructions and the coalgebra block are emitted and run) without forcing a
-- layer. Measured: `.text` 0x81b → 0xd2b.
useNu : Nu (K Int) -> Int
useNu = compose (\u -> 42) terminal

main : IO Unit
main = exit@S (useNu (mkNu 7))
